WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.822
You want to introduce FundsXML, as a sender or as a receiver of fund data.

2
00:00:04.872 --> 00:00:11.617
In this film you see how such a project runs, from the first idea to daily operation, and what helps along the way.

3
00:00:12.867 --> 00:00:13.881
Why start at all?

4
00:00:13.931 --> 00:00:16.823
The business case usually rests on four points.

5
00:00:16.873 --> 00:00:21.454
Fewer interfaces: one format for many partners instead of one per partner.

6
00:00:21.504 --> 00:00:27.212
Partners ask for it: distributors, platforms and insurers increasingly expect standard data.

7
00:00:27.262 --> 00:00:32.970
Regulation is built in: new templates arrive through the standard, not through new projects.

8
00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:38.791
And quality improves, because every file is checked automatically before it leaves the house.

9
00:00:40.141 --> 00:00:42.653
A FundsXML project runs in six phases.

10
00:00:42.703 --> 00:00:45.351
Preparation sets the goal and the scope.

11
00:00:45.401 --> 00:00:48.520
Mapping connects your own data to the standard.

12
00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:52.498
A prototype and a pilot prove that it works with real data.

13
00:00:52.548 --> 00:00:54.117
Testing makes it robust.

14
00:00:54.167 --> 00:00:56.342
Go-live moves it into production.

15
00:00:56.392 --> 00:00:59.983
And operations keep it running, release after release.

16
00:01:00.033 --> 00:01:02.276
Let us go through them one by one.

17
00:01:03.626 --> 00:01:06.175
Preparation starts with the question why.

18
00:01:06.225 --> 00:01:12.704
Which partners ask for FundsXML, and which data do they need: prices, portfolios, regulatory templates?

19
00:01:12.754 --> 00:01:16.507
Which version of the standard do they expect, and how often?

20
00:01:16.557 --> 00:01:20.500
Collect these requirements first, and agree on them in writing.

21
00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:25.001
A clear scope is the best protection against a project that never ends.

22
00:01:26.351 --> 00:01:28.485
Mapping is usually the biggest step.

23
00:01:28.535 --> 00:01:35.219
For every field you deliver, you decide where it comes from in your own systems, and where it goes in FundsXML.

24
00:01:35.269 --> 00:01:36.857
Some fields map one to one.

25
00:01:36.907 --> 00:01:42.135
Others need a conversion, for example from internal codes to the standard's code lists.

26
00:01:42.185 --> 00:01:44.502
And some data may not exist yet at all.

27
00:01:44.552 --> 00:01:49.537
A mapping table, reviewed by business and IT together, is the heart of the project.

28
00:01:50.887 --> 00:01:56.018
Then build a prototype, and start from the public example files instead of an empty page.

29
00:01:56.068 --> 00:02:03.178
Produce a first real delivery for one fund, check it against the schema and the business rules, and send it to one partner.

30
00:02:03.228 --> 00:02:10.512
A pilot with real data and one friendly partner shows quickly where the mapping is still wrong, long before the full roll-out.

31
00:02:11.862 --> 00:02:13.846
Testing makes the solution robust.

32
00:02:13.896 --> 00:02:20.129
Test the files against the schema and your business rules, with good data and with deliberately bad data.

33
00:02:20.179 --> 00:02:24.855
Test the special cases: a new share class, a fund in liquidation, a correction.

34
00:02:24.905 --> 00:02:30.061
And test end to end with your partners, until they confirm that they can load the data.

35
00:02:30.111 --> 00:02:34.189
Keep all test files: they are your safety net for every later change.

36
00:02:35.539 --> 00:02:38.632
Agree a few things with every partner, in writing.

37
00:02:38.682 --> 00:02:44.353
The scope and the version: which data, and which versions of FundsXML and of the templates.

38
00:02:44.403 --> 00:02:50.702
The rhythm and the timing: on which days files arrive, until what time, and how corrections are sent.

39
00:02:50.752 --> 00:02:57.302
The channel: how files travel, for example by secure file transfer, through an API or through a platform.

40
00:02:57.352 --> 00:03:00.571
And the contacts: who to call when a delivery fails.

41
00:03:01.921 --> 00:03:04.709
Go-live moves the deliveries into production.

42
00:03:04.759 --> 00:03:09.566
Automate them: the files are produced, checked and sent without manual steps.

43
00:03:09.616 --> 00:03:14.548
Monitor every delivery, and alert someone when a check fails or a file is late.

44
00:03:14.598 --> 00:03:21.675
Then the project becomes operations: new partners, new funds, and new versions of the standard and the templates.

45
00:03:21.725 --> 00:03:23.946
Plan for at least one update a year.

46
00:03:25.296 --> 00:03:30.144
If you are on the receiving side, the project looks similar, but the focus differs.

47
00:03:30.194 --> 00:03:32.977
Collect the files from all senders in one place.

48
00:03:33.027 --> 00:03:37.639
Check every file before loading it, against the schema and your business rules.

49
00:03:37.689 --> 00:03:42.714
Build one import that serves all fund houses, because they all use the same structure.

50
00:03:42.764 --> 00:03:48.911
And report findings back to the sender quickly, with a clear description, so the next delivery is better.

51
00:03:50.261 --> 00:03:51.831
Who needs to be involved?

52
00:03:51.881 --> 00:03:56.303
The business side knows what the data means and what partners expect.

53
00:03:56.353 --> 00:03:58.506
IT builds and runs the interfaces.

54
00:03:58.556 --> 00:04:01.098
Operations watches the daily deliveries.

55
00:04:01.148 --> 00:04:04.014
And the partners themselves test and confirm.

56
00:04:04.064 --> 00:04:09.393
Projects go best when business and IT own the mapping together, from the first day.

57
00:04:10.743 --> 00:04:12.260
Some pitfalls are typical.

58
00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:15.695
Starting too big, with all data and all partners at once.

59
00:04:15.745 --> 00:04:20.035
Treating it as an IT-only project, so the meaning of the data gets lost.

60
00:04:20.085 --> 00:04:23.952
Checking only against the schema, and not against business rules.

61
00:04:24.002 --> 00:04:29.738
And forgetting that the standard and the templates change, so nobody plans for the next version.

62
00:04:31.088 --> 00:04:32.685
What can you do next week?

63
00:04:32.735 --> 00:04:36.991
Ask your main partners which FundsXML data and versions they expect.

64
00:04:37.041 --> 00:04:41.678
Download the schema and the example files, and open them with a free tool.

65
00:04:41.728 --> 00:04:47.568
Pick one fund and one data area, and sketch the mapping with your business and IT colleagues.

66
00:04:47.618 --> 00:04:49.658
And find one partner for a pilot.

67
00:04:49.708 --> 00:04:52.635
Four small steps, and your project has started.

68
00:04:53.985 --> 00:04:56.612
Everything you need to start is free.

69
00:04:56.662 --> 00:05:00.013
The schema and the example files are on GitHub.

70
00:05:00.063 --> 00:05:02.980
The documentation is on fundsxml dot org.

71
00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:08.697
And free tools, such as FreeXmlToolkit, open, check and explore FundsXML files.

72
00:05:10.047 --> 00:05:11.871
How can this look in practice?

73
00:05:11.921 --> 00:05:16.742
Here is a typical first project of a mid-sized fund house, as an illustration.

74
00:05:16.792 --> 00:05:20.864
It starts small: daily prices and the EMT, for three distributors.

75
00:05:20.914 --> 00:05:24.050
Two weeks to agree the scope, four for the mapping.

76
00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:28.734
After about nine weeks, the first real file is accepted by one distributor.

77
00:05:28.784 --> 00:05:36.041
Testing with good and bad files takes three more weeks, and in week thirteen the deliveries go live, fully automated.

78
00:05:36.091 --> 00:05:40.288
The other two distributors follow in operation, with the same files.

79
00:05:41.638 --> 00:05:43.191
How do new versions fit in?

80
00:05:43.241 --> 00:05:47.289
Every file names the version it was written for, in its control data.

81
00:05:47.339 --> 00:05:52.930
New releases mostly add elements, and these are usually optional, so existing files stay valid.

82
00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:57.502
Partners agree on the version they exchange, and move on when they are ready.

83
00:05:57.552 --> 00:06:01.956
And every change is documented in the changelog that comes with the schema.

84
00:06:03.306 --> 00:06:05.145
FundsXML is a living standard.

85
00:06:05.195 --> 00:06:09.113
New versions follow new regulation and the needs of the market.

86
00:06:09.163 --> 00:06:17.867
Changes are discussed and agreed by the people who use the standard every day: asset managers, administrators, banks and service providers.

87
00:06:17.917 --> 00:06:25.425
And your voice counts: you can report issues and propose changes on GitHub, and every proposal is discussed in the open.

88
00:06:26.775 --> 00:06:28.702
And the standard keeps moving.

89
00:06:28.752 --> 00:06:37.073
Data is increasingly exchanged through interfaces and APIs, not only as files, and FundsXML documents fit well into that world.

90
00:06:37.123 --> 00:06:39.709
New regulation will bring new templates.

91
00:06:39.759 --> 00:06:45.773
And new tools, including AI-based ones, help with mapping, checking and analysing fund data.

92
00:06:45.823 --> 00:06:50.321
A clean, standard structure is the best starting point for all of it.

93
00:06:51.671 --> 00:06:54.176
That was a FundsXML project from A to Z.

94
00:06:54.226 --> 00:07:01.521
In the next film, the last one for business readers, you see how FundsXML evolves, and how you can take part in it.

95
00:07:01.571 --> 00:07:02.798
Thanks for watching.
